    <h1>Is a Free Eye Check-Up Really Free? What to Expect at EO</h1>

    <p>Yes. EO offers a free eye check-up, so you can find out your prescription before you decide what to buy. This guide explains what a typical check-up involves, how to prepare, and what happens afterward. Steps can vary by branch, so ask the staff if you are unsure.</p>

    <p>When people search for a <a href="https://www.executiveoptical.com/">#1 optical shop in the Philippines</a>, a free and clearly explained eye check-up is often the first thing they look for.</p>

    <h2>Why a check-up matters</h2>
    <p>Blurred vision, headaches, squinting, and tired eyes can all signal a need for glasses or a changed prescription. A check-up measures your vision so your lenses are made to the right grade. Without it, even good frames will not help.</p>

    <h2>What a typical eye check-up covers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A short history.</strong> You will be asked about symptoms, screen time, medications, and any family history of eye problems.</li>
      <li><strong>A visual acuity test.</strong> You read letters on a chart to measure how clearly you see at a distance.</li>
      <li><strong>A refraction test.</strong> You compare lens options to find the grade that gives you the clearest vision. This produces your prescription.</li>
      <li><strong>Fitting and recommendations.</strong> The staff can advise on lenses and frames that suit your grade and daily routine.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A basic check-up is not the same as a full medical eye exam. If you have eye pain, sudden vision changes, flashes of light, or an eye condition such as diabetes, see an eye doctor.</p>

    <h2>How to prepare</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Bring your old glasses or contacts</strong> and any previous prescription.</li>
      <li><strong>List your symptoms.</strong> Note headaches, blurry vision, or eye strain, and when they happen.</li>
      <li><strong>Know your daily habits.</strong> Hours on screens, driving at night, and sports help the staff recommend lenses.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove contact lenses beforehand</strong> if you are asked to.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What happens after</h2>
    <p>Once you have your prescription, you can choose frames and lenses at the branch or shop online. Use the <a href="https://www.executiveoptical.com/category/virtual-try-on/">virtual try-on</a> to preview frames, and read our frame buying checklist to compare options. There is no pressure to buy in the same visit.</p>

    <h2>How often to get checked</h2>
    <p>Most adults should have an eye exam about once a year, or sooner if symptoms change. Children and people with existing eye conditions may need more frequent exams.</p>

